1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:08,143
In December 2009, one of America's
biggest pizza chains did something almost

2
00:00:08,183 --> 00:00:09,183
unthinkable.

3
00:00:09,681 --> 00:00:13,934
It built an advertising campaign around
people criticizing its own food.

4
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:16,634
Customers complained about the crust.

5
00:00:17,254 --> 00:00:18,638
They attacked the sauce.

6
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:22,435
Some questioned whether the
pizza tasted fresh at all.

7
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:27,632
Instead of hiding those opinions, Domino's
put them at the center of its marketing.

8
00:00:27,683 --> 00:00:31,381
Then the company announced that
it had changed its pizza recipe.

9
00:00:31,843 --> 00:00:35,201
Within months, domestic
same-store sales surged.

10
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:39,857
How did admitting a product wasn't good
enough become part of an extraordinary

11
00:00:39,897 --> 00:00:41,367
corporate turnaround?

12
00:00:43,215 --> 00:00:45,972
Domino's began in Michigan in 1960.

13
00:00:46,642 --> 00:00:51,142
Over the following decades, the company
built its reputation around convenience,

14
00:00:51,472 --> 00:00:54,242
delivery and a repeatable franchise model.

15
00:00:54,651 --> 00:00:56,844
Customers knew what Domino's offered.

16
00:00:57,319 --> 00:01:01,730
Pizza brought directly to their doors,
often with an emphasis on speed and

17
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:02,770
consistency.

18
00:01:03,258 --> 00:01:07,672
That operational focus helped the chain
expand across the United States and

19
00:01:07,712 --> 00:01:08,712
internationally.

20
00:01:09,156 --> 00:01:13,390
But being good at delivery was not the
same as being loved for the food itself.

21
00:01:14,014 --> 00:01:18,511
As competitors fought for customers,
Domino's faced a difficult question.

22
00:01:18,949 --> 00:01:23,389
Had the company become better known for
how pizza arrived than for how it tasted?

23
00:01:25,229 --> 00:01:28,528
By the late 2000s, Domino's
had a perception problem.

24
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:33,369
Consumer research associated the company
with delivery convenience, but its pizza

25
00:01:33,409 --> 00:01:36,079
faced criticism for taste and quality.

26
00:01:36,495 --> 00:01:39,267
The broader pizza category
was also under pressure.

27
00:01:39,799 --> 00:01:43,758
Customers had more restaurant choices, and
competitors were fighting aggressively

28
00:01:43,798 --> 00:01:45,208
for their business.

29
00:01:45,806 --> 00:01:48,032
Domino's needed more
than another discount.

30
00:01:48,626 --> 00:01:52,088
It needed to give people a reason
to reconsider the product.

31
00:01:52,628 --> 00:01:57,121
The challenge was especially difficult
because many customers already believed they

32
00:01:57,161 --> 00:01:59,431
knew what Domino's pizza tasted like.

33
00:01:59,864 --> 00:02:03,831
Changing that opinion required
changing something real.

34
00:02:05,569 --> 00:02:10,103
The company examined customer feedback and
confronted comments that were unusually

35
00:02:10,143 --> 00:02:11,143
harsh.

36
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:16,082
Its campaign would later feature complaints
about the crust, sauce and overall

37
00:02:16,122 --> 00:02:17,352
eating experience.

38
00:02:17,906 --> 00:02:22,627
These were not flattering testimonials
selected to make the brand look successful.

39
00:02:23,274 --> 00:02:26,341
They represented the doubts
management wanted to overcome.

40
00:02:26,871 --> 00:02:30,754
But Domino's faced a problem
familiar to any struggling business.

41
00:02:31,332 --> 00:02:36,019
If a company announces that its product has
improved, why should skeptical customers

42
00:02:36,059 --> 00:02:37,149
believe it?

43
00:02:37,485 --> 00:02:40,194
The answer would require more
than reassuring language.

44
00:02:40,794 --> 00:02:45,160
Management decided to show the criticism
and make its response visible.

45
00:02:47,061 --> 00:02:49,019
Domino's made a fundamental choice.

46
00:02:49,682 --> 00:02:54,469
Instead of relying on marketing to improve
perceptions of the existing pizza, it

47
00:02:54,509 --> 00:02:57,619
reformulated its core hand-tossed product.

48
00:02:58,170 --> 00:03:04,131
The changes included a garlic-seasoned
crust, a more robust tomato sauce and a

49
00:03:04,171 --> 00:03:06,141
revised shredded-cheese blend.

50
00:03:06,677 --> 00:03:08,929
This was not a limited-time novelty.

51
00:03:09,512 --> 00:03:12,805
The company was changing the pizza
at the center of its business.

52
00:03:13,401 --> 00:03:17,757
That meant introducing the new recipe
across a large network of stores while

53
00:03:17,797 --> 00:03:20,707
maintaining consistency
and delivery performance.

54
00:03:21,383 --> 00:03:24,515
The decision was operational
as well as creative.

55
00:03:25,131 --> 00:03:27,240
Advertising could promise change.

56
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:29,707
The kitchens had to deliver it.

57
00:03:31,306 --> 00:03:33,610
Then came the more surprising decision.

58
00:03:34,191 --> 00:03:37,766
Domino's would tell customers why
the change had been necessary.

59
00:03:38,316 --> 00:03:42,215
Rather than pretending its previous pizza
had always been excellent, the company

60
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:44,505
publicly acknowledged negative feedback.

61
00:03:45,182 --> 00:03:49,037
The campaign turned criticism into
evidence that management had listened.

62
00:03:49,564 --> 00:03:50,564
It was risky.

63
00:03:50,915 --> 00:03:54,484
A viewer might remember the complaints
and ignore the improvements.

64
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:57,325
Competitors could benefit
from the admission.

65
00:03:57,955 --> 00:04:02,367
But Domino's was betting that honesty,
backed by a better product, would be more

66
00:04:02,407 --> 00:04:06,737
persuasive than another conventional
advertisement claiming great taste.

67
00:04:08,563 --> 00:04:14,050
On December twenty-seventh, 2009, the
reformulated pizza became available across

68
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:16,060
Domino's American stores.

69
00:04:16,676 --> 00:04:20,588
The Pizza Turnaround campaign brought
the product changes to public attention.

70
00:04:21,232 --> 00:04:25,599
Its documentary-style presentation showed
company personnel confronting criticism

71
00:04:25,639 --> 00:04:28,689
and explaining the new recipe.

72
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:33,557
The company also created a website
dedicated to the turnaround and customer

73
00:04:33,597 --> 00:04:34,597
feedback.

74
00:04:34,922 --> 00:04:36,467
The message was direct.

75
00:04:37,019 --> 00:04:41,561
Domino's had heard what people disliked,
changed the food and wanted another chance.

76
00:04:42,237 --> 00:04:45,373
It was not asking customers
to forget the past.

77
00:04:45,995 --> 00:04:48,939
It was asking them to judge the
new product for themselves.

78
00:04:51,045 --> 00:04:55,956
The campaign generated attention, but
publicity alone was not the objective.

79
00:04:56,603 --> 00:05:01,182
Domino's needed people to visit,
order and try the reformulated pizza.

80
00:05:01,806 --> 00:05:06,406
More importantly, the company needed customers
to return after that first purchase.

81
00:05:06,923 --> 00:05:11,589
Management reported encouraging traffic
and sales momentum following the launch.

82
00:05:12,175 --> 00:05:17,013
Still, the first weeks could not establish
whether the turnaround would last.

83
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:22,340
A major promotion and a heavily publicized
product change can produce temporary

84
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:23,380
excitement.

85
00:05:23,932 --> 00:05:27,635
The more meaningful test would come when
the company reported comparable-store sales

86
00:05:27,675 --> 00:05:30,145
for the first quarter of 2010.

87
00:05:32,125 --> 00:05:36,743
On May fourth, 2010, Domino's
announced its first-quarter results.

88
00:05:37,390 --> 00:05:41,841
Domestic same-store sales had increased
fourteen point three percent compared with

89
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:42,951
the previous year.

90
00:05:43,651 --> 00:05:47,994
Company-owned American stores rose
fourteen point seven percent.

91
00:05:48,034 --> 00:05:52,179
Domestic franchise stores increased
fourteen point two percent.

92
00:05:52,219 --> 00:05:56,874
The company attributed the overall improvement
to increased traffic following the

93
00:05:56,914 --> 00:05:58,724
introduction of its new pizza.

94
00:05:59,313 --> 00:06:03,080
The result was extraordinary for
an established restaurant chain.

95
00:06:03,608 --> 00:06:06,182
But the number needs to
be understood correctly.

96
00:06:06,832 --> 00:06:11,303
It measured sales growth at comparable
stores, not a fourteen percent increase in

97
00:06:11,343 --> 00:06:12,343
corporate profit.

98
00:06:14,096 --> 00:06:17,385
The campaign challenged a
familiar advertising assumption.

99
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:21,822
Brands usually emphasize strengths
and minimize weaknesses.

100
00:06:22,521 --> 00:06:27,062
Domino's did the opposite, using
criticism to explain why customers should

101
00:06:27,102 --> 00:06:28,572
reconsider its product.

102
00:06:29,047 --> 00:06:30,000
The strategy connected three things.

103
00:06:31,583 --> 00:06:33,068
A recognized problem.

104
00:06:33,597 --> 00:06:34,832
A visible response.

105
00:06:35,589 --> 00:06:38,852
And a specific change
customers could experience.

106
00:06:39,496 --> 00:06:43,579
That made the message more credible
than a vague promise of improvement.

107
00:06:44,205 --> 00:06:48,096
But the confession would have meant
little without the reformulated pizza.

108
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:54,123
The campaign succeeded as a demonstration
of change, not merely as an exercise in

109
00:06:54,163 --> 00:06:55,693
self-deprecating humor.

110
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:02,295
Changing a core product is dangerous because
established customers may prefer the

111
00:07:02,335 --> 00:07:03,335
original.

112
00:07:03,401 --> 00:07:08,509
A large franchise system also needs consistent
ingredients, preparation procedures

113
00:07:08,759 --> 00:07:09,789
and product quality.

114
00:07:10,399 --> 00:07:15,381
Domino's had to coordinate its new recipe
across thousands of American locations.

115
00:07:16,111 --> 00:07:21,584
Its promise depended on what customers
actually received, not just what appeared in

116
00:07:21,624 --> 00:07:22,624
advertisements.

117
00:07:23,211 --> 00:07:27,930
A weak execution could have reinforced
the criticism the company had amplified.

118
00:07:28,646 --> 00:07:31,617
This is why the decision
mattered beyond marketing.

119
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:37,970
Domino's aligned the product, operations
and message around one strategic objective.

120
00:07:38,591 --> 00:07:41,867
Convince customers the
pizza itself had improved.

121
00:07:43,762 --> 00:07:47,095
Domino's was also strengthening
other parts of its business.

122
00:07:47,642 --> 00:07:52,283
The company had invested in online
ordering, technology and a highly developed

123
00:07:52,323 --> 00:07:53,493
delivery network.

124
00:07:54,026 --> 00:07:58,294
These capabilities made it easier for
interested customers to act on the new

125
00:07:58,334 --> 00:07:59,334
campaign.

126
00:07:59,631 --> 00:08:05,305
In 2010, Domino's reported that online
orders represented a substantial share of

127
00:08:05,345 --> 00:08:10,575
sales, with some stores receiving much
higher digital-order volumes than others.

128
00:08:11,051 --> 00:08:14,576
The recipe overhaul was therefore
not happening in isolation.

129
00:08:15,391 --> 00:08:19,915
Product improvement attracted attention,
while the company's ordering and delivery

130
00:08:19,955 --> 00:08:23,245
infrastructure helped turn that
attention into transactions.

131
00:08:25,393 --> 00:08:27,984
The improvement continued
beyond the first quarter.

132
00:08:28,498 --> 00:08:32,924
Domino's reported domestic same-store sales
growth of eight point eight percent in

133
00:08:32,964 --> 00:08:37,854
the second quarter of 2010 and eleven
point seven percent in the third.

134
00:08:38,381 --> 00:08:43,376
These figures suggested that the new product
and supporting strategy were producing

135
00:08:43,416 --> 00:08:45,806
more than a brief launch-week spike.

136
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:51,239
They did not prove that the advertising
campaign alone caused every additional sale.

137
00:08:51,752 --> 00:08:56,950
Pricing, promotion, customer traffic,
operations and broader business conditions

138
00:08:56,990 --> 00:08:58,280
also mattered.

139
00:08:58,713 --> 00:09:03,139
But the results showed that the company had
achieved a substantial shift in domestic

140
00:09:03,179 --> 00:09:04,309
sales momentum.

141
00:09:06,194 --> 00:09:11,188
Domino's later became widely recognized
for its digital ordering capabilities,

142
00:09:11,318 --> 00:09:13,848
delivery systems and global expansion.

143
00:09:14,453 --> 00:09:19,421
Those achievements developed over years
and cannot be credited exclusively to one

144
00:09:19,461 --> 00:09:20,931
advertising campaign.

145
00:09:21,539 --> 00:09:25,819
The Pizza Turnaround was an important
early chapter because it demonstrated that

146
00:09:25,859 --> 00:09:29,769
management was willing to challenge
assumptions about the core product.

147
00:09:30,288 --> 00:09:35,258
But the company's longer-term success
required sustained investment and execution.

148
00:09:36,118 --> 00:09:37,614
This distinction matters.

149
00:09:38,239 --> 00:09:40,268
A campaign can change the conversation.

150
00:09:41,034 --> 00:09:45,153
A business must keep delivering on the
promise long after the advertisements

151
00:09:45,193 --> 00:09:46,193
disappear.

152
00:09:47,938 --> 00:09:50,544
The evidence points to
four connected decisions.

153
00:09:51,327 --> 00:09:54,761
First, Domino's took customer
criticism seriously.

154
00:09:55,368 --> 00:09:59,522
Second, it changed the product rather
than merely changing the message.

155
00:10:00,102 --> 00:10:04,889
Third, it made the criticism public, turning
skepticism into the foundation of its

156
00:10:04,929 --> 00:10:05,929
campaign.

157
00:10:06,129 --> 00:10:10,425
And fourth, it supported the launch through
a delivery and ordering system capable

158
00:10:10,465 --> 00:10:12,415
of handling renewed demand.

159
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:16,892
None of these choices alone explains
the company's entire future.

160
00:10:17,394 --> 00:10:22,181
Together, they created a credible reason
for customers to try Domino's again.

161
00:10:22,657 --> 00:10:26,979
The turnaround began when management stopped
defending the old product and committed

162
00:10:27,019 --> 00:10:28,229
to improving it.

163
00:10:29,906 --> 00:10:33,673
Domino's made a decision many
brands would consider unthinkable.

164
00:10:34,277 --> 00:10:38,551
It publicly admitted that customers had
serious problems with its most important

165
00:10:38,591 --> 00:10:39,591
product.

166
00:10:39,710 --> 00:10:41,686
But the admission was only the beginning.

167
00:10:42,217 --> 00:10:47,192
The company changed its recipe, showed
customers what it had done and gave them a

168
00:10:47,232 --> 00:10:48,482
reason to reconsider.

169
00:10:49,123 --> 00:10:52,859
The lesson is not that every brand
should advertise its weaknesses.

170
00:10:53,681 --> 00:10:58,102
It is that honesty becomes powerful when
it is supported by meaningful action.

171
00:10:58,703 --> 00:11:02,535
Domino's didn't improve simply
by saying its pizza was bad.

172
00:11:02,999 --> 00:11:07,239
It improved the pizza and made that
decision impossible to ignore.

173
00:11:07,781 --> 00:11:09,680
And every brand leaves evidence.

